examples.question_text:
The diagram shows a square with sides of $1$ m.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black]
\draw (0,0) rectangle (3,3);
\path (1.5,0) node[below] {$1\text{ m}$};
\path (0,1.5) node[left] {$1\text{ m}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
(a) Use the side lengths in centimetres to find how many square centimetres are in $1\text{ m}^2$.
(b) A hectare is a square with sides of $100$ m. Express $1$ ha in square metres.
(c) A square kilometre is a square with sides of $1000$ m. Express $1\text{ km}^2$ in square metres.
(d) State whether to multiply or divide, and by what factor, when converting $\text{cm}^2$ to $\text{mm}^2$ and $\text{m}^2$ to ha.

examples.solution_text:
(a) Each side is $100$ cm.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black]
\draw (0,0) rectangle (3,3);
\path (1.5,0) node[below] {$100\text{ cm}$};
\path (0,1.5) node[left] {$100\text{ cm}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
$100\times100=10\,000$, so $1\text{ m}^2=10\,000\text{ cm}^2$.
(b) $100\times100=10\,000$, so $1\text{ ha}=10\,000\text{ m}^2$.
(c) $1000\times1000=1\,000\,000$, so $1\text{ km}^2=1\,000\,000\text{ m}^2$.
(d) From $\text{cm}^2$ to $\text{mm}^2$, multiply by $10^2=100$: the destination unit is smaller.
From $\text{m}^2$ to ha, divide by $100^2=10\,000$: the destination unit is larger.
